  "evidence": "TEXT_A: Rapid zero‑carbon transition; no new oil and gas; stop and phase out nuclear; rising carbon tax; public ownership of rail/water/Big 5 energy firms; £15 minimum wage and move toward a four‑day week; rent controls and 150,000 social homes; proportional representation and path to rejoin the EU; cancel Trident. TEXT_B: Halve employee NI and abolish main rate NI for the self‑employed; no rises in income tax or VAT; legal annual cap on migration and Rwanda flights; NHS funding above inflation with more nurses/doctors and tech upgrade; 1.6m homes with Help to Buy and private renting reform; tougher sentencing and 8,000 more neighbourhood police; North Sea oil and gas licensing plus new gas power stations; defence to 2.5% of GDP.",
  "reasoning": "Polling shows voters’ top concerns are cost of living/tax, the NHS, immigration and border control, housing, crime and defence—areas directly and repeatedly prioritised in B’s commitments. A emphasises systemic climate transformation, public ownership and constitutional reform, which are less aligned with the electorate’s primary salience compared to B’s focus.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "B"
}
